  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2, drawn at half height. It is 3 times steeper in x (left-right), so overshoot shows there first. With no noise, steps in x grow once the learning rate is above 2/3.",
    "Each step moves the ball by −learning rate × (true slope + noise). The noise is centred on zero, and its size is 2·√((64−b)/(63·b)) for batch size b, which is 0 at b = 64. It comes from a fixed seed, so Reset replays it.",
    "Both arrows are steps (learning rate × slope), drawn at 2 times their true length on one shared scale. The purple arrow includes noise, and the dark one uses the full data. When a run ends, both are hidden and the step length reads 0.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished), or when the loss goes above 6, its value at the steep rim (diverged). The ball and path are then held at the bowl's edge, while the readout keeps the true loss.",
    "Run plays 5 steps per second. With noise, a single step can raise the loss; it falls only on average."
  ]
}